t0 Auto-compilation Principle




- What :
    Produce a compilation result from the sources AND the result itself.
-


- Why :
    Guarantee that the production of the result is ONLY based on \
internal mechanisms of the used sources.
-


- How :
    1) Use external mechanisms 100% controled to produce a first version. \
Then, from this first result, generate the second one (that is, \
actually, the first generated one).

    2) Make use of manual compilation of the producer, and then use it to \
generate the second version (That is, as well, the first generated \
one).
-


- Absurd :
    1) In all cases, we need the result we want to produce before it \
exists (as initial item).

    2) Even if we had the result as initial item, this one must be the \
same as the one we want to generate so the generation seems \
useless as it will not bring anything more to it.
-


- Feasibility :
	Besides the 2 absurdities given above, this auto-compilation \
guarantee based on internal mechanisms is POSSIBLE. If it is \
possible to represent schematically internal mechanisms serving for \
producing a result and this representation is consistent, then any \
algorithmic operator (that can be human, material, virtual...) is \
able to carry out a first compilation that would respect EVERY \
internal mechanism at 100%. Therefore, the second absurdity is \
closed as long as the main objective is preceded by an implicit \
principle : "Having a result that would be useable in the same \
FORMAT than the one targetted.". Indeed, if a human wrote on a \
paper all the operations that an executable file should do, it \
would not become useless to create the executable file that would \
fit the same role now.
-


- Strategy :
	1) For limitating at maximum rate the differences related to the \
used mechanisms, it is preferable to use the MANUAL method \
because this one does not require any external supply of \
knowledge (gain of time and complexity).

	2) To also understand better and ajust the internal mechanisms of \
the producer, using MANUAL compilation allow fixing, modeling \
and re-architecturing the targetted structure faster, the ins \
and outs.

	3) The "right to make a mistake" is essential. More than just a \
permission, it is the MEAN by which we can guarantee respecting \
the mechanisms used. By comparing the result from a manual \
compilation and an automatic one, we can adjust more easilly on \
one side or on the other one depending on the targetted result : \
Either the manual operator has made a mistake, or a mechanism has \
an imprevisible behavior to be fixed.
-

